6. ACCOUNTING ENGINE
========================================================

The accounting engine is the most important component
of this application.

Implement a true double-entry bookkeeping system.

Every posted journal entry must contain:

- Unique journal identifier.
- Transaction date.
- Accounting period.
- Reference number.
- Source document.
- Description.
- Debit account lines.
- Credit account lines.
- Currency.
- Exchange rate when applicable.
- Amounts.
- Created by.
- Approved by, where applicable.
- Posting timestamp.
- Transaction status.
- Audit references.

CORE ACCOUNTING RULE:

For every posted journal entry:

TOTAL DEBITS = TOTAL CREDITS.

Reject any journal entry that does not balance.

Do not allow a user to post a transaction with missing
accounts, invalid amounts, or an invalid accounting period.

Use decimal-safe monetary calculations.

Do not use ordinary floating-point arithmetic for financial
amounts without appropriate precision controls.

Store monetary amounts using fixed-precision decimal types
where supported.

Define and consistently apply currency precision.

Store the original transaction currency, functional currency,
and exchange rate where foreign currency is supported.

Do not silently change previously posted accounting entries.

If a posted transaction requires correction, create an
auditable reversal or adjustment.

Support these transaction states:

- Draft
- Pending Approval
- Approved
- Posted
- Reversed
- Rejected

Only authorised users may approve or post transactions.

A posted journal must not be deleted or silently edited.

Maintain a complete record of every correction.

